      "parameters": {
        "width": 1000,
        "height": 340,
        "content": "## upsert-patient / find-patient\nupsert-patient forwards straight to its sub-workflow. find-patient loads context, looks up the phone by exact match (Always Output Data, so no match still yields one empty item), drops that placeholder, and wraps whatever's left into { rows }, which is [] for a first-time patient."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000026",
      "name": "Sticky Note2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        360,
        1360
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 2440,
        "height": 1120,
        "content": "## cleanup\nRuling P6: a phone_prefix must match ^\\d{8,}$ (digits only, 8+) or the request is refused with prefix_too_short before anything runs — refused means nothing is deleted. Each of the four parts (Calendar events, Sheets rows, inbound_messages, n8n_chat_histories) has its own On Error continue, so one failing part doesn't block the others; a failed part reports null and ok is true only when all four succeeded. Calendar and Sheets match the prefix with a literal, case-sensitive substring/startsWith check in a Filter node (never a SQL/regex pattern built from the prefix), and the two Postgres deletes use starts_with($1) — never LIKE, which a prefix like \"________\" would turn into a wildcard that deletes every row. Sheets rows are sorted by row_number descending before deleting one at a time, so deleting a lower row first can't shift the row numbers of the ones still queued."
      }
    }
